{"kind":"report","text":"Signed local-signer index/migration pre-Source decision at Main a0cfba4febb81bc9d2a3a8bb890a6a78ee1fca04, under69a33abc. Proceed to separately reviewed derived-index Source under the conditions below. No public integration or Source approval.\n\nEquivalence: latest_local_signer(actor PK,seq,time), scoped to one exact incarnation/index version, is appropriate because sealed history time is nondecreasing. The last signed entry by sequence is therefore also that actor's latest qualifying time; compare inclusively time\u003e=since, after the same request signature/target/resource/clock/notAfter/window checks as today. Preserve exact signerOf: act and preparation signers, and only applied founding genesis; deliveries/outcomes/diagnoses/other genesis do not acquire a signer. Do not repurpose entry.actor: SqliteStore.append currently leaves it null, and accepted/idempotency bookkeeping is not this consumer. Use the shared signer function, avoiding a Store↔signed-read module cycle if needed by factoring pure classification.\n\nAppend/index update must share the existing sealing transaction with entry/fold writes (Turn#seal); rollback leaves neither one ahead. Reject stale/wrong generation rows, seq beyond current head and inconsistent time/entry metadata rather than granting from them. Highest-seq upsert prevents backfill overwriting a newer maintained fact. Changes to the signer policy/index version require explicit invalidation/rebuild; current scope incarnation cannot borrow a previous generation's positive row.\n\nReadiness/disclosure: internally distinguish positive-proved, complete-negative, and not-ready/broken. A validated native append or validated contiguous backfill prefix may supply positive eligibility before full backfill, with the current time/window checked again per read. A missing or too-old row before ready is UNKNOWN, not an authoritative local-negative and not permission to use a broad causal-history fallback. An independently completed eligible genesis-root proof may still establish summary-equivalent scope authority; do not require current membership instead. A known migration inconsistency/corruption stops this proof path, not a successful partial completion.\n\nPublic callers with only a valid signed request have authenticated a key, not proved read authority. Keep unfinished/unsupported/unavailable proof indistinguishable from forbidden for such callers: no not-ready/head/incarnation/cutoff/progress/density detail, and no new generic proof-unavailable response that only existing scopes emit. A proven authorized operator may see bounded private migration diagnostics through an explicitly authorized existing surface; not through a new universal signed error. Do not return an empty successful page or not-found for an unknown eligibility result. Normal clock refusal semantics remain as adopted; no broadened disclosure is introduced here.\n\nDuring initial Source prep the old public summary path remains unchanged and signed query stays disabled. Later integration must consume the tri-state result without falling back to the current unbounded history scan. A constant-size denial alone is not a proof that timing side channels are absent; make no stronger timing claim without evidence.\n\nMigration: no constructor history traversal. Initialize only bounded schema/control data and capture a durable fixed legacy cutoff seq/hash plus full incarnation/index version. Backfill from genesis by contiguous seq in bounded PK order. Persist next expected seq, previous hash/time and cutoff atomically with each batch's index updates; reaching a maximum observed seq or a row count is not complete. Validate actual canonical entry bytes/hash, full own incarnation, seq, prev and nondecreasing time; establish genesis and finish at the captured cutoff hash. Missing, malformed, inconsistent or unsupported rows stop explicitly. An empty new store must become ready only via the normal creation/index path, not a marker that accidentally certifies an existing prefix.\n\nUse128rows/1MiB as maxima per maintenance quantum, including validation and all row-size/body admission; fixed control/SQL/metadata/copy costs need their own bounded model. Read truthful bounded byte metadata BEFORE record bodies (the direct-column primitive/runtime proof from G2a or another verified bound); do not treat legacy stored size alone as protection against a corrupt oversized record, or fetch128 arbitrary bodies before counting. A legal row larger than a quantum must stop as unfit/unsupported with operator-visible reason, not be skipped or create an endless empty retry. Reuse existing bounded entry/type validation and pure hashing; no replay judgments, resolver fetches, native entries, dispatch or provider operation is part of backfill.\n\nAll post-cutoff appends maintain the same-generation index atomically from the start. Completing the fixed legacy prefix plus this continuously maintained tail establishes ready; tail appends do not move the cutoff forever. Check generation/head consistency around control transitions. Crash/reopen must resume committed progress, replay a rolled-back batch safely and preserve newer signer rows. No mutable request-body or time cache grants a read later without fresh checks.\n\nOwner/scheduling: the deployed scope owns maintenance, through the existing Wakes aggregator in outbox.ts. Add a separate durable maintenance wake/progress, included in the minimum with deadline, dispatch, nextSend and in-memory driver wake. Do NOT call raw setAlarm independently, store the maintenance deadline in native timed state, or let completion/deleteAlarm erase another owner's wake. Wakes.deadline currently persists its own deadline and recomputes the minimum; preserve that property for every owner's schedule/clear.\n\nBootstrap a pending maintenance wake at bounded object lifecycle initialization/reopen; no authenticated read is required to start it and no read performs a backfill quantum. Existing dormant objects migrate when activated unless a separately commissioned host-wide sweep activates them; do not claim deployment alone walks every object. Run at most one bounded maintenance quantum per alarm activation with explicit fair ordering relative to native drain/dispatch/operations, and recompute all wakes after every pass/error. In particular do not put maintenance exclusively after an unbounded-until-empty/native async owner and then claim it cannot starve. Conversely a backlog must not loop through all batches before serving native owners. Yield and re-arm at a finite retry delay, preserving earlier wakes. A permanently invalid row stops/quarantines maintenance rather than hot-looping forever or blocking unrelated owner scheduling. This scheduling changes no native entry or outside-attempt retry rule.\n\nWitnesses: every signerOf variant and time-boundary equivalence; maintained positives before ready, unknown negatives never causal/global authority; no unauthorized readiness/head leak; unbounded old scan not called by the new lookup; wrong generation/head/version refused; backwards/missing/corrupt/cutoff mismatch stops; byte/row/object-work bounds before body reads; append/backfill and late-old upsert interleavings; atomic rollback, crash/reopen and ready transition; first activation scheduling, earlier native alarm preservation, independent wake clearing, bounded fairness and malformed-row no-hot-loop behavior. Genesis-root preparation still has its own byte/step/time bound and remains owed.\n\nStatic evidence: exact signerOf/checkLocalSignedRead/checkSignedRead and Chains.update, SqliteStore.append/storedFrom, Turn#seal/#wake, deployed object alarm/#rooted, Wakes arbitration. Existing Chains.update loops over all new/retry roots; this index does not make that path bounded by itself. No project execution, service operation or mutation besides this workroom decision.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:544b6213fbb3a6d6284e7fdb2ea3883f0c304779","scope":"pre-Source local-signer index/migration decision"}}